WEBVTT

1
00:00:00.760 --> 00:00:02.320
Hello, I'm Marc Donovici.

2
00:00:02.360 --> 00:00:06.180
I'm an IT auditor from Strasbourg,
France, and I'm here to present RUNSAFE,

3
00:00:06.220 --> 00:00:09.120
my design software that uses
new mathematical theory

4
00:00:09.160 --> 00:00:14.050
to give knitwear a damage rating and see
how far one dropped stitch can run.

5
00:00:14.260 --> 00:00:19.130
The goal is to have small faults stay
repairable and garments stay in use.

6
00:00:19.848 --> 00:00:22.108
Your phone has a water rating,

7
00:00:22.148 --> 00:00:25.938
your sunscreen has an SPF,
but your sweater has nothing.

8
00:00:26.128 --> 00:00:29.438
No one can tell how far
a dropped stitch will run.

9
00:00:29.808 --> 00:00:32.808
Here are two sections of the same sleeve.

10
00:00:32.848 --> 00:00:36.728
In plain knitting, you can see
that the ladder runs right to the edge.

11
00:00:36.768 --> 00:00:39.988
But with RUNSAFE,
it stops within nine loops.

12
00:00:40.028 --> 00:00:43.178
And that limit is proved
in a mathematical way.

13
00:00:44.254 --> 00:00:48.154
In my job, I check whether claims hold up.

14
00:00:48.194 --> 00:00:50.694
So I looked at a fine knit top.

15
00:00:50.734 --> 00:00:54.894
A stitch dropped at the elbow
can ladder 22 cm to the cuff,

16
00:00:54.934 --> 00:00:57.944
and an almost intact top is thrown away.

17
00:00:58.054 --> 00:01:00.924
So I wanted a rating anyone could check.

18
00:01:02.174 --> 00:01:03.734
The idea is quite simple.

19
00:01:03.774 --> 00:01:07.534
Every eight rows,
RUNSAFE knits a barrier course,

20
00:01:07.574 --> 00:01:11.054
where each loop is held
by two loops instead of one.

21
00:01:11.094 --> 00:01:15.194
When a ladder reaches it,
the second holder stops it.

22
00:01:15.234 --> 00:01:17.744
And the proof doesn't rely on friction.

23
00:01:18.643 --> 00:01:21.103
The hard part is the proof.

24
00:01:21.143 --> 00:01:26.703
Up to nine stitches can drop from this
sleeve in a 33-digit number of ways.

25
00:01:26.743 --> 00:01:31.473
No lab can test them all,
so I proved the worst case for every one.

26
00:01:31.743 --> 00:01:34.783
First, the fabric:
one dropped stitch opens

27
00:01:34.823 --> 00:01:41.073
at most one segment between barriers,
and nine open at most 45, at any size.

28
00:01:41.403 --> 00:01:45.203
Second, the machine:
the program adds loops of its own,

29
00:01:45.243 --> 00:01:47.483
and the limit holds even when they fail.

30
00:01:47.523 --> 00:01:55.433
And third, the cost: from 155 times
the machine passes, down to 1.31.

31
00:01:56.143 --> 00:01:58.643
And I checked it all
with Carnegie Mellon's

32
00:01:58.683 --> 00:02:02.773
knitting simulator and over
800,000 exhaustive tests.

33
00:02:03.614 --> 00:02:06.434
So a designer no longer draws barriers.

34
00:02:06.474 --> 00:02:11.104
They state the rating:
one dropped stitch within a centimetre,

35
00:02:11.194 --> 00:02:16.604
nine under 450 loops,
and at most 35% more knitting.

36
00:02:17.074 --> 00:02:22.064
RUNSAFE returns the cheapest proved
construction: a barrier every eight rows.

37
00:02:22.394 --> 00:02:25.434
Ask for 300 loops,
and nothing fits the budget.

38
00:02:25.474 --> 00:02:29.334
Allow 50%, and it switches
to staggered barriers,

39
00:02:29.374 --> 00:02:34.584
compiled, verified and exported
as a Shima Seiki machine file.

40
00:02:38.344 --> 00:02:40.604
The rating goes on the care label.

41
00:02:40.644 --> 00:02:44.884
Scan it, and your own phone
downloads the knitting program,

42
00:02:44.924 --> 00:02:48.134
rebuilds the loops
and checks the proof again.

43
00:02:48.204 --> 00:02:53.814
A brand, a repairer or a buyer can verify
it without taking my word for it.

44
00:02:55.134 --> 00:02:57.034
If a stitch drops,

45
00:02:57.074 --> 00:03:01.764
the Studio shows which loops were
released and lists the ones to re-form.

46
00:03:01.954 --> 00:03:06.074
How repairers restore barrier loops
is one of my first tests.

47
00:03:06.114 --> 00:03:11.664
And the sleeve stays one continuous yarn,
ready to unravel at the end of its life.

48
00:03:13.224 --> 00:03:15.114
So, where does it go?

49
00:03:15.384 --> 00:03:17.734
Fine knitwear in smooth yarns first.

50
00:03:17.864 --> 00:03:24.094
Next, tights, the classic ladder:
130 million pairs a year in France alone.

51
00:03:24.904 --> 00:03:28.324
Then, a damage rating
in digital product passports.

52
00:03:28.364 --> 00:03:33.224
And the climate case: the barrier repays
its extra knitting once it prevents

53
00:03:33.264 --> 00:03:39.314
1.3% of discards, even if only half of
the longer wear replaces a new purchase.

54
00:03:40.872 --> 00:03:45.942
My three biggest challenges are
physical validation, repair and adoption.

55
00:03:46.152 --> 00:03:47.652
First, I need a knitting laboratory

56
00:03:47.692 --> 00:03:52.922
to test the limits on real fabrics
after washing and wear.

57
00:03:53.172 --> 00:03:57.012
Second, I need repair specialists to show
how barrier loops can be restored,

58
00:03:57.052 --> 00:03:58.452
and at what cost.

59
00:03:58.492 --> 00:04:01.812
And third, I need a brand partner
to judge the look,

60
00:04:01.852 --> 00:04:04.142
the production time
and the willingness to pay.

61
00:04:04.492 --> 00:04:07.892
The grant would fund these trials
and give me time to lead them.

62
00:04:07.932 --> 00:04:10.772
And the Changemaker Programme
would connect me

63
00:04:10.812 --> 00:04:14.532
with the people who can turn
a proved design into knitwear people keep.

64
00:04:14.572 --> 00:04:15.572
Thank you.
